You are given an integer array height of length n. There are n vertical lines drawn such that the two endpoints of
the ith line are (i, 0) and (i, height[i]).
Find two lines that together with the x-axis form a container, such that the container contains the most water.
Return the maximum amount of water a container can store.
Notice that you may not slant the container.
https://leetcode.com/problems/container-with-most-water/description/
Example 1:
Input: height = [1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7]
Output: 49
Explanation: The above vertical lines are represented by array [1,8,6,2,5,4,8,3,7]. 
In this case, the max area of water (blue section) the container can contain is 49.
Example 2:
Input: height = [1,1]
Output: 1
 
Constraints:
n == height.length
2 <= n <= 105
0 <= height[i] <= 104

=============================================
The area is always limited by the shorter line.
Left height = 3
Right height = 8
Water height = 3, not 8.

Start from the widest container
Left pointer at start (0)
Right pointer at end (n - 1)
This gives maximum width.

Rule:Move the pointer with the smaller height
Why?
Area depends on min(height[left], height[right])
Moving the taller line won’t help
Only moving the shorter line gives a chance for a taller height
This is the core logic.

C# Brute Force Code
=====================
public class Solution
{
    public int MaxArea(int[] height)
    {
        int n = height.Length;
        int maxArea = 0;

        // Pick first line
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        {
            // Pick second line
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
            {
                int width = j - i;
                int minHeight = Math.Min(height[i], height[j]);
                int area = width * minHeight;

                if (area > maxArea)
                {
                    maxArea = area;
                }
            }
        }

        return maxArea;
    }
}

Try every pair of lines
Calculate how much water they can hold
Keep the maximum
==============================================================

Optimal
=======
public class Solution {
    public int MaxArea(int[] height) {
        int left=0;
        int right= height.Length-1;
        int maxArea=0;
        while(left<right){
            int width= right-left;
            int hight=Math.Min(height[left],height[right]);
            int area=width*hight;
            maxArea=Math.Max(area,maxArea);

            if(height[left]<height[right]){
                left++;
            }
            else{
                right--;
            }
        }
        return maxArea;
    }
}

==========================================
We move the pointer at the shorter line because the current area is limited by it. 
Moving the taller line cannot increase the area, but moving the shorter one might find a taller boundary.

We use two pointers starting at both ends to maximize width. At each step, the area is limited by the shorter height. 
To potentially increase area, we move the pointer at the shorter height,
since moving the taller one cannot improve the area. This results in an O(n) solution.
